Semaphores:
=============
   • There are 2 types of Semaphores :
        ⦿ Binary Semaphores
            » A Binary Semaphore restricts access to exactly one thread at a time.
            =======================================================================
            » The following code creates a binary semaphore with a single permit
              Semaphore binarySemaphore = new Semaphore(1);

              Example of Fair semaphore:
              --------------------------
              Semaphore s1 = new Semaphore(int permits, true)
              Semaphore s2 = new Semaphore(3, true);

              » Guarantees FIFO (First-In-First-Out) order.
              » Threads are granted permits based on the exact sequence they requested access
              » Prevents starvation but introduces a slight performance overhead.
            ========================================================================
            » It behaves similarly to a mutual exclusion lock (Mutex), functioning with only two valid states: 0 or 1 available permit
            » It is initialized with exactly 1 permit. When a thread acquires the permit, the count drops to 0, locking the section from all other threads.


       ⦿ Counting Semaphores:
           » A Counting Semaphore allows multiple threads to access a shared resource simultaneously up to a predefined limit.
           » It is initialized with a specific number of "permits" (N > 1)
           » Each time a thread successfully calls acquire(), the permit count decreases by one.
           » If the permit count reaches zero, any subsequent threads attempting to gain access will block until an existing thread calls release() to return a permit


   Use cases of Semaphores:
   ========================
       »  API Rate Limiter
       »  Parallel Task Limit